{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/SKILL.md && rg -n -i -C 2 \\\"P57B|P57A|P57C|shared\\\" <original-home>/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | head -100 && sed -n '1,180p' PLAN.md && sed -n '1,180p' work/TWO-LANE-FORMALIZATION-DECISION.md && sed -n '1,260p' aristotle_tasks/P57B-vacuum-itp-completion.md && sed -n '1,240p' reviews/P57A-vacuum-restricted-itp-core-closeout.md && sed -n '1,200p' SOURCE-CONTRACT.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/SKILL.md && rg -n -i -C 2 \\\"P57B|P57A|P57C|shared\\\" <original-home>/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | head -100 && sed -n '1,180p' PLAN.md && sed -n '1,180p' work/TWO-LANE-FORMALIZATION-DECISION.md && sed -n '1,260p' aristotle_tasks/P57B-vacuum-itp-completion.md && sed -n '1,240p' reviews/P57A-vacuum-restricted-itp-core-closeout.md && sed -n '1,200p' SOURCE-CONTRACT.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: aristotle-cli\ndescription: Use when Codex should interact with the Aristotle CLI to submit Lean projects or papers, request sorry filling or formalization runs, inspect job status, and download results for iterative project work.\n---\n\n# Aristotle CLI\n\nUse Aristotle for remote Lean/project runs when the local repo is ready but needs automated proof search, sorry filling, or formalization help.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the target project or paper from the current workspace.\n2. Check the local build or project state first. Prefer a narrow local `lake build` or equivalent before submitting anything remote.\n3. Use the bundled wrapper `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh` unless `ARISTOTLE_API_KEY` is already known to be exported in the current process.\n4. Submit the smallest useful task prompt. Keep it concrete and tied to the files at hand.\n5. Wait for the result when that is useful. Otherwise poll or list jobs.\n6. Download artifacts, inspect the diffs, and rerun local verification.\n\n## Commands\n\nUse the commands in [references/aristotle-cli.md](references/aristotle-cli.md).\n\n## Operating rules\n\n- Prefer `aristotle submit` for Lean project work, especially sorry filling or proof search.\n- Prefer `aristotle formalize` when the input is a paper or TeX source that should become a Lean artifact.\n- Use `aristotle list` to inspect recent jobs and `aristotle download` to retrieve results.\n- Keep prompts specific: name the file, theorem, section, or proof obligation instead of asking for broad improvement.\n- If a run fails, inspect the local artifact and re-submit only after the prompt or project state is corrected.\n- Do not print or log the API key.\n- If authentication fails but `~/.bashrc` contains the key, do not assume sourcing `.bashrc` works. Many `.bashrc` files return early for noninteractive shells. Use `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n\n## When to escalate back to the user\n\n- The CLI is missing, or authentication still fails after trying `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n- The target repo is not a Lean project or paper and needs clarification.\n- The requested action would overwrite user work outside the target project tree.\n745-- Lean, mathlib, StarAlgEquiv, isUnitalTracial_pullback, TraceTransport.lean, tracial-state, semifinite-trace, II-infinity, hyperfinite-II1, `congrArg tau (e.map_mul ...)`\n746-\n747:## Task 2: retire partial P56 and launch P56R/P57A recovery tracks\n748-\n749-### rollout_summary_files\n750-\n751:- rollout_summaries/2026-08-02T19-10-40-cRYC-standalone_stabilizer_hyperfinite_formalization_and_itp_reco.md (cwd=source-workspace, rollout_path=<original-home>/.codex/sessions/2026/08/02/rollout-2026-08-02T21-10-40-019fc3e2-abbe-71f2-9c08-db9f0ba1e6f2.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-10T06:29:45+00:00, thread_id=019fc3e2-abbe-71f2-9c08-db9f0ba1e6f2, partial: P56 retired; P56R/P57A queued for semantic recovery)\n752-\n753-### keywords\n754-\n755:- P55A, P55D, P56, ProductStateBound, P56R, P57A, P57, bound_beating, InfiniteTensorProductHilbert.lean, PreInnerProductSpace.Core, Aristotle, aristotle-autopilot.timer\n756-\n757-## Task 3: dependency-gate automation and the topology/Kaplansky sublane\n--\n779-- `Formalization/TraceTransport.lean` proves unital tracial-functional pullback along `StarAlgEquiv` without project-specific axioms. Verify with `lake env lean Formalization/TraceTransport.lean`, `lake build Formalization.TraceTransport`, a two-pass `pdflatex` blueprint build, and an axiom audit expecting only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound`. [Task 1]\n780-- P55A–P55D are accepted concrete-alpha prerequisites. P56 proves finite-word/algebraic identities only: its C*-extension depends on introduced `ProductStateBound` and lacks positivity, so it is not a formal C*-state. [Task 2]\n781:- `bound_beating/formalize_itp/Formalization/InfiniteTensorProductHilbert.lean` contributes an auditable finite-support kernel, finite-mode tensor realization, positivity, sesquilinearity, and `PreInnerProductSpace.Core`; exclude `VacuumRestrictedITPInnerProductAxioms` and completion wrappers from accepted evidence. P56R targets finite-density-matrix-derived boundedness/positivity, P57A a local audited ITP-core port, and P57 waits on both. [Task 2]\n782-- `P48` constructs the decreasing projection tower and strong-limit `q`, retaining `q ∈ M` under explicit lattice/support/representation hypotheses via commutant closedness and `M=(M')'`; it does not prove the analytic double-commutant theorem. Pinned Mathlib has WOT primitives and generic strong convergence, but defines concrete `VonNeumannAlgebra` by double commutant and lacks the analytic SOT/WOT equivalence and Kaplansky density package. [Task 3]\n783-- Preserve the dependency `P49` finite-stage/C*-corner trace → bicommutant/Kaplansky bounded approximation → `P50` faithful normal trace on `qMq` → `P51` finiteness/fullness. The finiteness calculation needs `τ_q(q-vv*)=0`; `q-v*v` is identically zero when `v*v=q`. Initial ready packets were `P47, P48, P52, P54`; recheck live state before relying on that snapshot. [Task 3]\n--\n973-# Task Group: <original-home>/Projects/current/ml_engineer Python recursion, backtracking, and call-local state explanations\n974-\n975:scope: explain recursive Python code at the level of per-call stack frames, local bindings, closure-shared state, and mutability, using `generateParenthesis` as the concrete backtracking case\n976-applies_to: cwd=<original-home>/Projects/current/ml_engineer; reuse_rule=reuse the scope/mutability explanation for beginner Python recursion questions; inspect the supplied code before assuming whether a variable is local, closed over, or mutated\n977-\n--\n989-\n990-- when the user asks how “always the same string `s` is manipulated” or says they do not understand “namespace” -> explain call-local namespaces/stack frames explicitly, with a short frame trace, rather than only giving a recursion tree [Task 1]\n991:- for syntax/algorithm questions that reveal a missing mental model -> contrast the relevant categories concretely (here local `s`, `left`, `right` versus closure-shared mutable `res`) before returning to the algorithm [Task 1]\n992-\n993-## Reusable knowledge\n994-\n995-- Each invocation of `backtrack` gets distinct local bindings for `s`, `left`, and `right`. The name is reused, but `backtrack(s + '(', left + 1, right)` passes a new value to the child frame. [Task 1]\n996:- Python strings are immutable: `s + '('` creates a new string and cannot alter the caller's `s`. By contrast, `res` belongs to the enclosing `generateParenthesis` scope, is found through closure lookup, and is one shared list mutated by `res.append(s)`. [Task 1]\n997:- A minimal explanatory trace is root `s=\"\"` -> child `s=\"(\"` -> child `s=\"((\"`; show it alongside the shared `res` to answer the scope question directly. [Task 1]\n998-\n999-## Failures and how to do differently\n1000-\n1001:- symptom: a correct high-level backtracking explanation still does not answer the question -> cause: it describes branches but not the user's model of names, frames, and immutable objects -> fix: show a short call-frame trace and distinguish per-call local values from closure-shared mutable state. [Task 1]\n1002-\n1003-# Task Group: <original-home>/Projects/current/qm_lean/qubit_pres Lean qubit Hilbert-space setup and foundational type-theory teaching\n--\n1780-## Reusable knowledge\n1781-\n1782:- `_posts/2026-07-23-untitled.md` uses `layout: single`, `date: 2026-07-23`, `section: musings`, and `published: false`; the site uses Markdown/kramdown, MathJax, and footnote citations. The accepted structure is `The issue`, `A shared representation of knowledge`, then `A concrete realization` [Task 1]\n1783-- Verify an unpublished draft without modifying tracked `_site` via `bundle exec jekyll build --unpublished --destination /tmp/homepage-draft-verify-20260723` [Task 1]\n1784-- The separate spec is `drafts/typed-scientific-argument-system-spec.tex`. An `ArgumentArtifact` has one primary conclusion and Lamport-addressed steps; each step has finite explicit inputs/assumptions/bindings, one output `LocalStatement`, transformation kind, justification, and status. Atomicity means an independent reviewer can check whether the declared transformation produces that one output from the stated inputs. [Task 2]\n--\n2388-- The correct local skill roots for this machine are under `<original-home>/.codex/skills/`; stale paths with an extra `r0/` component were wrong for `send-via-telegram` and `proof-council` [Task 2]\n2389-- Lamport rewriting fits naturally as a post-acceptance sibling artifact: keep `answer.tex` canonical, write `lamport_proof.tex` plus `solutions/<problem_id>-lamport.tex`, and expose readiness/compile/report fields separately so Lamport failures do not invalidate the accepted proof [Task 3]\n2390:- The shared Author file-block parser ignores non-canonical outputs, so non-canonical artifacts like `lamport_proof.tex` need a dedicated parser rather than reuse of the canonical Author parser [Task 3]\n2391-- The repo's `problems/` directory is mixed tracked fixtures plus local ad hoc problem statements, so the safe ignore rule is `problems/*.txt`; verify it with `git check-ignore -v` before committing [Task 4]\n2392-\n# Active plan: two-lane stabilizer-algebra formalization\n\n## Decision — 2026-08-07\n\nThe project now has two parallel mathematical targets sharing finite\nstabilizer and operator-algebra infrastructure, but neither is allowed to\nsilently supply the other one's hypotheses.\n\n### Lane G — general lattices and central type-III exclusion\n\n**Source basis:** `main.tex:332-342`, with the corner proof at\n`main.tex:1026-1536`.\n\nFor a general consistent stabilizer system and region, construct the decreasing\nlocal-code projection tower, its strong limit `q`, and the finite corner\n`q M_Gamma q`. Prove that the vector functional `tau_q` is a faithful normal\ntracial **state** on that corner, hence that `q` is finite; prove `q` is full;\nand derive that every nonzero central projection `z` has `zq != 0` finite in\n`z M_Gamma`. Conclude that no nonzero central summand is type III.\n\nThis lane does **not** require a direct-integral formalization or a global\ntrace/weight on `M_Gamma`. A global faithful normal semifinite weight is a\nseparate optional strengthening. The condition to formalize is the existence\nof a nonzero finite **projection/corner** in each nonzero central summand, not\nmerely a finite-dimensional subalgebra.\n\n### Lane H — explicit local-hyperbolic tensor-factor model\n\n**Source basis:** `main.tex:1671-1679`, `main.tex:1898-2004`.\n\nFrom a coherent local hyperbolic basis, construct the finite coordinate maps,\ntheir coherent algebraic direct-limit star equivalence, concrete C-star\nmodels, the state identity, and the product GNS tensor splitting. Then prove\nthe special regional algebra is spatially isomorphic to\n\n`B(K_pure) barTensor R_mixed`.\n\nAfter proving the two factors and their spatial tensor product, derive the\ntrivial-center/factor conclusion for this special case. This conclusion does\nnot follow merely from the word \"hyperbolic\"; it follows from the constructed\nspatial factorization.\n\n### Reconnection\n\nLane H gives an explicit factor theorem and, in the infinite-pure case, the\nconcrete faithful normal semifinite weight `Tr barTensor tau`. Lane G gives\nthe general no-central-type-III theorem. A final comparison theorem may show\nthat the Lane-H factor model satisfies the Lane-G finite-corner criterion, but\nthe two results remain logically independent.\n\n## Aristotle package sequence\n\n### Shared/central core\n\n| Packet | Lean target | Purpose and acceptance gate |\n|---|---|---|\n| P47 | `CenterFiniteProjectionCore.lean` | Define center, central projections, finite projections, corners, central support, and fullness for the concrete von Neumann-algebra model. Prove only algebraic order/corner lemmas; no direct integral. |\n| P48 | `GeneralLatticeProjectionTower.lean` | Formalize the general-lattice exhaustion, local code projections, monotonicity, and the strong-limit projection `q`. If the stabilizer definitions do not imply a required limit fact, expose it as an auxiliary hypothesis. |\n| P49 | `FiniteCornerTrace.lean` | Derive finite-stage trace cyclicity and pass it to the compressed quasi-local C-star algebra. Keep finite codespace density data explicit. |\n| P49A | `StrongOperatorClosedVonNeumann.lean` | Develop the strong-operator convergence/closure API needed for concrete operator algebras; prove commutants and hence concrete von Neumann algebras are strongly closed. This is a proved infrastructure theorem, not an interface. |\n| P49B | `VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean` | Prove the analytic double-commutant theorem for a unital complex star subalgebra of bounded operators: its bicommutant is its strong-operator closure. Develop the required finite amplification/cyclic-subspace machinery locally when Mathlib lacks it. |\n| P49C | `KaplanskyDensity.lean` | Prove bounded/unit-ball Kaplansky density for the represented C-star algebra in its bicommutant and derive the compressed-corner approximation used by the manuscript. No supplied density interface is accepted. |\n| P50 | `NormalFaithfulCornerTrace.lean` | Using P49 and P49C, prove traciality of `tau_q` on `q M_Gamma q` by bounded strong approximation, prove faithfulness, and establish the stronger concrete WOT-continuity certificate that P50U will translate into the standard ultraweak-normal interface. |\n| P50U | `UltraweakNormality.lean` | Define the concrete ultraweak topology from absolutely summable matrix-coefficient series, prove the ultraweak-to-WOT identity is continuous, and upgrade P50's WOT-continuity certificate to literal ultraweak normality. This is mandatory infrastructure and may not alias ultraweak topology to WOT/SOT/norm. |\n| P51 | `FullFiniteCornerCentralExclusion.lean` | Prove `q` finite, prove fullness, and derive `0 != z ->` a nonzero finite projection in `z M_Gamma`; define/use the project-local type-III obstruction. |\n\n### Explicit local-hyperbolic lane\n\n| Packet | Lean target | Purpose and acceptance gate |\n|---|---|---|\n| P52 | `CoherentLocalHyperbolicCoordinates.lean` | Build finite coordinate blocks and an exhaustion from local hyperbolic data. Audit whether finite support suffices; otherwise isolate the exact local-finiteness/coherence assumption. |\n| P53 | `LocalHyperbolicAlgebraicAlpha.lean` | Construct the finite stage maps, phase calibration, and coherent algebraic `alpha`; instantiate the existing direct-limit theorems without supplied stage equivalences. |\n| P54 | `UHFReferenceRepresentation.lean` | Construct a faithful compatible Hilbert/operator representation of the dyadic algebra from finite stages; do not assume an infinite tensor-product Hilbert space. |\n| P55A | `LocalHyperbolicFiniteStageUHFBridge.lean` | Construct, rather than assume, the finite-stage star equivalence from each P53 coordinate Pauli algebra to the corresponding finite dyadic matrix algebra, with explicit generator/phase formulas and norm control. |\n| P55B | `LocalHyperbolicSourceUHFBridge.lean` | Prove coherence of the P55A maps and identify the P53 source direct limit with the coordinate dyadic limit; prove the finite terminal-stage and infinite cofinal-UHF branches separately. |\n| P55C | `LocalHyperbolicConcreteRepresentations.lean` | Transport the P54 faithful representation through P55B and P53 alpha, and construct the source and regional closed operator-range C-star algebras with faithful dense embeddings. |\n| P55D | `RegionalConcreteCStarAlpha.lean` | Extend the P53 algebraic alpha uniquely between the P55C concrete C-star closures and prove the inverse and dense-range identities. |\n| P56 | `RegionalProductStateIdentity.lean` | Retired partial result: it proves the finite-word/algebraic state equality and conditional continuous-extension uniqueness, but does not prove C-star boundedness or positivity. |\n| P56R | `RegionalProductStateIdentity.lean` | Recovery after P55D: construct the pure/mixed product C-star state from finite-dimensional density-matrix estimates, prove positivity/unitality and transport it through `alpha`; no supplied norm bound. |\n| P57A | `VacuumRestrictedInfiniteTensorProduct.lean` | Shared infrastructure: port and audit the proved finite-support vacuum-restricted infinite-tensor pre-Hilbert core from `bound_beating`; do not import external projects or package a completion as an axiom. |\n| P57 | `ProductGNSTensorFactorization.lean` | After P54, P56R, and P57A, construct the pure/mixed product GNS Hilbert-space tensor splitting and the spatial represented-algebra identification. |\n| P58 | `MixedUHFHyperfiniteFactor.lean` | Construct/prove the finite mixed matrix and infinite mixed hyperfinite-II1 alternatives under project-local factor and normal-trace definitions. |\n| P59 | `LocalHyperbolicFactorTraceTheorem.lean` | Retired partial transport artifact: it compiles conditional centre/trace/weight transports but introduces unproved bridge and local-normality premises, so it is not evidence for the Lane-H theorem. |\n| P59A | `ConcretePureMixedProductData.lean` | Construct the pure/mixed coordinate C-star subalgebras, their commuting dense product decomposition, and the concrete `ProductGNSData` instance directly from P55D/P56R. |\n| P57B | `VacuumRestrictedITPCompletion.lean` | Starting from P57A's proved finite-support core, construct the null-space quotient/completion as an actual Hilbert space with dense algebraic embedding; no completed ITP interface may be assumed. |\n| P57C | `PureGNSFactor.lean` | Identify the concrete pure component of P59A with the P57B vacuum product representation and prove its represented von Neumann closure is the full `B(K_pure)` factor. |\n| P59B | `MixedGNSUHFBridge.lean` | Identify P59A's mixed GNS representation with P58's dyadic-UHF model and transport its factor/tracial structure; no fresh mixed-factor premise. |\n| P59R | `RegionalFactorTransport.lean` | Combine P57, P57C, and P59B to identify the regional represented algebra with the concrete spatial product and prove trivial centre/factorhood. |\n| P59T | `RegionalTraceWeight.lean` | Separately prove the finite-pure tracial-state and infinite-pure semifinite-weight results using a standard ultraweak-normal interface; never use P59's local vector-normal predicates as substitutes. |\n\n### Optional strengthening/reconnection\n\n| Packet | Lean target | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| P60 | `FullCornerSemifiniteWeight.lean` | Retired partial attempt: it constructs a conditional extended-positive trace series and finite-compression certificate, but does not yet prove standard positive-cone semifiniteness. |\n| P60R | `FullCornerSemifiniteWeight.lean` | Recovery: define the weight on the positive cone and prove standard semifiniteness using positive square roots and the finite comparison projections. |\n| P61 | `HyperbolicImpliesCentralCriterion.lean` | Show the explicit Lane-H model supplies the finite-corner criterion used by Lane G. |\n\n## Initial parallel wave\n\nSubmit only the four independent foundation packets first: P47, P48, P52,\nand P54. Integrate and semantically audit them before submitting dependent\npackets. Existing P42--P46 task drafts are preserved as historical drafts;\nthey are not evidence and must be re-scoped against this plan before reuse.\n\n## Non-negotiable gates\n\n1. No claim that `q M_Gamma q` has merely a semifinite weight when the\n   construction actually targets a finite faithful normal tracial state.\n2. No claim of a global trace/weight on `M_Gamma` from the finite-corner\n   theorem alone.\n3. No claim that a finite-dimensional subalgebra excludes type III; the proof\n   must produce a nonzero finite projection in every nonzero central summand.\n4. No claim that hyperbolic data imply a factor before the spatial tensor\n   factorization and factorhood of both factors are proved.\n5. Every manuscript-facing packet identifies an exact `main.tex` locator and\n   every extra analytic hypothesis appears as a named field, never an axiom.\n6. P49A--P49C are mandatory proved infrastructure. P50 may not replace the\n   bicommutant theorem or Kaplansky density by a supplied structure field; an\n   incomplete analytic package halts Lane G and triggers review.\n7. P50's WOT-continuity theorem is a mathematically stronger certificate but\n   is not itself the standard definition of normality. P50U must formalize the\n   actual ultraweak topology and checked comparison before the project reports\n   the corner state as ultraweak-normal in the formal interface. P51's\n   finiteness/fullness proof is logically independent because it uses only\n   traciality and faithfulness.\n8. The original P55 exhausted its remote budget without producing its\n   authorized Lean file. It is retired, not accepted. P55A--P55D replace it\n   and expose the previously missing bridge between P53's twisted-Pauli direct\n   limit and P54's dyadic UHF model. P56 may advance only after P55D is\n   integrated and audited.\n9. P56 is preserved as a partial artifact, not accepted evidence: its\n   `ProductStateBound` is an explicit missing C-star norm estimate and it has\n   no positivity theorem. P56R must derive the bound and positivity from the\n   finite-dimensional density-matrix data and P55D's concrete C-star model;\n   it may not use `ProductStateBound` in its final declarations or proofs.\n10. P57A may reuse the algorithmic finite-support construction in\n    `bound_beating/formalize_itp`, but only by an audited local port. It may\n    not import that separate project, copy its optional completion/axiom\n    wrappers, or claim a completed Hilbert tensor product before the quotient\n    and completion API is actually proved.\n11. P59 is a preserved partial transport artifact, not an accepted theorem.\n    P59A must construct, rather than assume, the `ProductGNSData` used by P57;\n    P59B must connect that datum to P58's `mixedVonNeumann`; and P57C must\n    establish the pure type-I factor. P59R may use only those proved bridges.\n    The finite/infinite trace conclusions are separated into P59T, where the\n    normality predicate must be standard ultraweak normality and the infinite\n    branch must carry an explicit infinitude hypothesis.\n\n---\n\n# Previous single-lane plan: retained background\n\n## Objective\n\nFormalize, from the manuscript's actual finite-support adapted-coordinate data,\na state-preserving C-star isomorphism from the quasi-local Pauli algebra to the\nregional algebra.  Obtain the represented von Neumann algebra equivalence by\nthe canonical GNS unitary, then apply an independently established product\nfactor classification.\n\nThe source basis is exclusively `SOURCE-CONTRACT.md` and the cited manuscript\nlocators.  In particular, no gLU/refinement/layer-coherence premise is allowed\nunless later added explicitly as a new auxiliary assumption.\n\n## Current position\n\nThe project already has checked conditional interfaces for:\n\n1. finite Pauli/stabilizer coordinates and pure/mixed states;\n2. algebraic direct limits, their induced algebraic equivalence `alpha`, and\n   compatible state transport;\n3. supplied C-star completions and a supplied C-star extension of `alpha`;\n4. GNS transport for supplied compatible C-star states; and\n5. supplied normal/product-factor transport.\n\nThese are scaffolding.  They do not discharge the manuscript-specific finite\ncoordinate construction or the product-factor classification.\n\n## Phase A — exact finite coordinate theorem\n# Two-lane formalization decision\n\nDate: 2026-08-07\n\nThe active Codex/Aristotle loop follows the two-lane plan in `PLAN.md`.\n\n**Lane G** formalizes the manuscript's general-lattice corner argument:\nthe strong-limit code projection `q`, the faithful normal finite tracial state\non `q M_Gamma q`, finiteness and fullness of `q`, and the conclusion that each\nnonzero central summand has a nonzero finite projection and hence is not type\nIII. This route does not use a direct integral and does not require a global\ntrace/weight on `M_Gamma`.\n\n**Lane H** formalizes the explicit short-range/local-hyperbolic model:\ncoherent coordinate maps, an algebraic and C-star `alpha`, the product-state\nidentity, product GNS tensor factorization, and the resulting factor and\ntrace/weight classification.\n\nThe lanes meet only after their own gates are met. In particular, the explicit\ntensor-factor result proves trivial center in the special local-hyperbolic\ncase, whereas the general corner result rules out type-III central summands\nwithout asserting trivial center.\n\nThe first approved parallel Aristotle wave is P47, P48, P52, and P54. No\ndependent packet should be submitted before its prerequisite results are\nintegrated and semantically audited.\n\nAmendment, 2026-08-07: the analytic passage from the compressed quasi-local\nC-star algebra to the von Neumann corner is mandatory project infrastructure.\nAfter P48, the loop must prove strong closedness of concrete von Neumann\nalgebras (P49A), the analytic double-commutant theorem (P49B), and Kaplansky\nunit-ball density with its compressed-corner corollary (P49C). P50 depends on\nboth the finite-stage/C-star trace P49 and the proved P49C result. These core\nanalytic theorems may require new local topology machinery, but may not be\nreplaced by axioms or supplied interfaces.\n\nAmendment, 2026-08-08: P50's checked WOT continuity is retained as a stronger\nconcrete certificate, but the field name alone is not accepted as a\nformalization of the standard ultraweak definition of normality. Package P50U\nmust define the ultraweak topology from absolutely summable series of matrix\ncoefficients, prove the topology comparison, and export an explicitly\nultraweak-normal P50 conclusion. This does not block P51's finite-projection\nargument, which uses the corner state's traciality and faithfulness rather than\nnormality.\n\nAmendment, 2026-08-08: the original P55 is retired after Aristotle returned\n`OUT_OF_BUDGET` without creating `RegionalConcreteCStarAlpha.lean`. The failed\nrun is not evidence and no code from it is integrated. Lane H now uses four\nseparate gates: P55A constructs finite-stage Pauli-to-dyadic equivalences,\nP55B proves coherence and the finite/infinite direct-limit identifications,\nP55C constructs faithful source/regional operator representations and their\nclosed ranges, and P55D extends the algebraic alpha by density. This split is\nmathematical: P53 and P54 did not yet contain an identification between their\ntwo different algebraic direct limits. P56 depends on accepted P55D.\n\nP60 may run independently of Lane H after P51 and P50U. It is optional Lane-G\nstrengthening and must use P50U's literal ultraweak-normal corner state. It may\nassume an explicitly named countable comparison family for the full corner,\nbut it may not infer a global weight from fullness alone or conceal the weight,\nnormality, faithfulness, traciality, or semifiniteness laws in an axiom.\n\nAmendment, 2026-08-08: P60 compiled and its conditional weight-series,\ntraciality, faithfulness, corner restriction, and finite-compression results\nare preserved, but P60 is not accepted as the optional global-weight theorem.\nThe closeout's P51 provenance objection was stale: P51 is accepted in\n`reviews/P51-full-finite-corner-central-exclusion-recovery.md`. The genuine\nremaining gap is standard semifiniteness on the positive cone. P60R may revise\nthe P60 candidate in place, but must prove that for each nonzero positive\n`x ∈ M` there is a nonzero positive `y ≤ x` of finite weight. It must construct\nthe required positive square-root/factorization argument from Mathlib or\nlocal proved C-star theory; that factorization may not be a new hypothesis.\n\nAmendment, 2026-08-10: P56 is retained as a partial, reviewed artifact. Its\nfinite-word and algebraic direct-limit identities are useful, but its concrete\nextensions depend on `ProductStateBound` and no positivity theorem was proved.\nP56R replaces it as the Lane-H state gate and must derive a genuine unital\npositive C-star functional directly from the finite pure/mixed density-matrix\ndata and the P55D concrete alpha. The original P56 job is disabled rather than\nrewritten, so the distinction remains machine-visible.\n\nIn parallel, P57A ports the *proved* finite-support vacuum-restricted infinite\ntensor pre-Hilbert construction from the compatible Lean-4.28 `bound_beating`\nworkspace into this repository as shared infrastructure. This is a local port,\nnot a cross-project import. Its optional completion/axiom wrapper is explicitly\nexcluded: P57A can establish the algebraic kernel, finite-mode realization,\npositivity, sesquilinearity, and pre-inner-product core, but the completed\nHilbert-space and spatial tensor claims remain P57 work and must be proved\nagainst the local Mathlib API.\n\nAmendment, 2026-08-10: P59 is retained only as an audited partial artifact.\nIt restated P57's generic transport but did not construct a `ProductGNSData`\nfrom the P55D/P56R local-hyperbolic model, did not identify that datum's mixed\nGNS representation with P58's `mixedVonNeumann`, and replaced the required\nfactor and trace inputs with fresh premise bundles. The recovery is therefore\nsplit into proof gates: P59A constructs the concrete product datum; P57B\nconstructs the actual Hilbert completion of the P57A vacuum core; P57C proves\nthe pure type-I factor; P59B proves the mixed-UHF bridge; and P59R transports\nthe resulting factorization to the regional algebra. P59T treats traces and\nweights only after P59R, using standard ultraweak normality and an explicit\ninfinite-pure hypothesis for the weight branch.\n# P57B — vacuum-restricted ITP completion\n\n## Context declaration\n\n- **Infrastructure only:** complete the proved finite-support vacuum-restricted\n  pre-inner-product core from P57A. This packet makes no manuscript factor or\n  trace claim.\n\n## Plan alignment\n\n- **Package and lane:** `P57B`; Lane `shared`.\n- **Predecessors:** `P57A`, integrated and locally audited.\n- **Gate discharged:** construct an actual quotient/completion Hilbert space\n  with dense algebraic vacuum-tensor image, so later pure-sector work does not\n  rely on an assumed infinite tensor-product Hilbert space.\n- **Forbidden downstream claims:** no identification with a pure GNS\n  representation, no `B(K)` closure/factor theorem, no mixed factor, no\n  spatial von Neumann product, and no trace/weight statement.\n\n## Mathematical target\n\nCreate only `AristotleCandidates/VacuumRestrictedITPCompletion.lean`. Starting\nwith `AristotleCandidates.VacuumRestrictedITP.itpCore`, use the pinned Mathlib\npre-inner-product construction to define the seminormed quotient implicit in\nthe core and its uniform-space completion. Prove that the resulting type has\nthe required complete normed complex inner-product/Hilbert structure, that the\nalgebraic vacuum-tensor span maps linearly and isometrically modulo zero into\nit with dense range, and that the vacuum image has norm one.\n\nThe quotient must be genuine: equal zero-norm vectors must be identified by\nthe construction, not retained in a merely seminormed space while calling it a\nHilbert space. Establish every transferred scalar, norm, inner-product, and\ncompletion instance by proof from P57A and Mathlib. If a required Mathlib API\nis absent, expose the exact missing theorem and leave this gate unaccepted;\ndo not introduce a completion structure, quotient, Hilbert instance, or dense\nembedding as a field, axiom, or interface.\n\n## Auxiliary assumptions\n\nExactly P57A's generic assumptions: `[DecidableEq ι]`, complex inner-product\nspaces `E i`, and unit reference vectors `Ω i`. No definiteness, quotient,\ncompletion, density, GNS, or operator-algebra premise may be added.\n\n## Files and imports\n\nCreate only `AristotleCandidates/VacuumRestrictedITPCompletion.lean`; import\nP57A and the necessary pinned Mathlib completion modules. Do not import\n`bound_beating`, edit P57A, or use its excluded completion wrappers.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `lake build AristotleCandidates.VacuumRestrictedITPCompletion` succeeds.\n- The final completion is an actual complete complex inner-product/Hilbert\n  space, with a proved dense algebraic map and proved vacuum norm one.\n- The module has no `sorry`, `admit`, axiom, opaque completion interface, or\n  claim about GNS/factor/spatial tensor products.\n- The response states how null vectors are quotiented and names every\n  nontrivial Mathlib completion theorem used.\n# P57A closeout review — vacuum-restricted infinite-tensor pre-Hilbert core\n\n## Decision\n\n**Status: proved at the stated shared-infrastructure scope; queue may advance.**\n\nAristotle task `563e769a-0e9b-4395-9895-e03c81c26851` was downloaded as\n`aristotle_responses/P57A-vacuum-restricted-itp-core-205100d0-563e769a.tar.gz`\nand extracted, without a build, dependency update, or fetch, only under\n`aristotle_responses/P57A-vacuum-restricted-itp-core-205100d0-563e769a-quarantine/`.\nOnly the packet-authorized module\n`AristotleCandidates/VacuumRestrictedInfiniteTensorProduct.lean` was\nintegrated into the live project.  The raw archive and quarantine extraction\nremain intact response records.  No manuscript file was changed.\n\nThe quarantined response incorrectly said that the nominated local source\n`<original-home>/Projects/current/bound_beating/formalize_itp/Formalization/InfiniteTensorProductHilbert.lean`\nwas absent.  It is present.  Local comparison confirms that the integrated\nmodule independently re-expresses the source's proved finite-support core,\nbut with a finite-Gram-coordinate proof route rather than importing the other\nproject.  I corrected that provenance comment in the live integrated module;\nthis changes documentation only.  The candidate and quarantine SHA-256 values\ntherefore differ:\n\n- live: `c4936227e370cd0d04c32f4d62807003ed688afba9ca661a330c696a5c75feca`;\n- quarantined response: `ee8862347e606cc8ac629e3baa8909354ad288da9a4f5502c8639fa595633c52`.\n\n## Source, assumptions, and exact declarations\n\nThis is **infrastructure only**, not a manuscript-facing assertion.  The\ncandidate imports only `Mathlib`; it does not import the `bound_beating`\nproject or any other package result.\n\nAll generic results use only the packet-authorized assumptions\n`[DecidableEq ι]`, `[∀ i, NormedAddCommGroup (E i)]`, and\n`[∀ i, InnerProductSpace ℂ (E i)]`.  The normalization premise\n\n```lean\nhΩ : ∀ i, ⟪Ω i, Ω i⟫_ℂ = 1\n```\n\nis used exactly where padding a product outside active support, positivity,\nfinite-mode kernel realization, finite-mode evaluation, and the exported\npre-inner-product core require it.  No completion, density, positivity, or\nexternal analytical premise was added.\n\nThe load-bearing live declarations are:\n\n- `exists_gram_coords` (lines 64--77), finite coordinates for every finite\n  Gram matrix;\n- `exists_finiteMode_realization` (88--104) and\n  `sum_prod_inner_nonneg` (109--122), realizing the product kernel as a\n  norm square in a finite coordinate tensor model;\n- `VacuumTensor` (128--134), `kern` (153--154), and\n  `kern_eq_prod_of_subset` (158--164), defining the finite-support\n  elementary tensors and support-independent kernel;\n- `itpInner` (183--184), `conj_itpInner_symm` (220--231),\n  `itpInner_add_left` (234--247), and `itpInner_smul_left` (250--262),\n  the algebraic sesquilinear extension;\n- `itpInner_nonneg` (266--285), positivity for an arbitrary finite linear\n  combination, and `re_itpInner_nonneg` (288--291);\n- `itpCore` (295--301), the exported\n  `PreInnerProductSpace.Core ℂ (ITPSpan Ω)`;\n- `vacuumVector` and `itpInner_vacuumVector` (309--322), whose\n  norm-square is one; and\n- `exists_finiteMode_realization_kern` (329--341) and\n  `itpInner_eq_finiteMode` (344--352), the finite-mode statements for the\n  actual vacuum-restricted kernel and algebraic span.\n\n## Acceptance review\n\n| Classification | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| proved | The finite-support elementary tensors, their finite-product kernel, the free finite span, sesquilinearity, finite-mode realization, positivity on arbitrary finite sums, the pre-inner-product core, and the algebraic vacuum norm-square-one theorem are all explicit proved declarations. |\n| partial | Quotienting the null space, completion, and an infinite tensor-product Hilbert-space instance are deliberately absent. |\n| blocked | None at P57A scope.  The local Mathlib snapshot lacks a ready-made inner-product API for a general `PiTensorProduct`; the candidate avoids that gap by proving the finite Gram-coordinate realization in `EuclideanSpace`, which suffices for the stated kernel positivity theorem. |\n| failed | None. |\n\nThe packet's mathematical target is met at the stated scope.  The changed\nroute is the finite-Gram-coordinate proof of positivity, instead of importing\nor copying an optional completion wrapper from the source workspace.  It adds\nno premises and neither weakens the algebraic target nor supplies a\ncompletion.\n\n## Plan alignment\n\n- **Package and lane:** P57A, Lane `shared` — correct.\n- **Predecessors:** none — correct.  The module is self-contained and imports\n  only Mathlib; it uses no package result that was merely remotely finished.\n- **Permitted gate:** discharged: it supplies a proved finite-support\n  vacuum-restricted algebraic ITP kernel and pre-inner-product core, so P57\n  need not recreate that algebraic layer.\n- **Forbidden downstream claims:** respected.  The module has no quotient or\n  completion, no completed infinite tensor-product Hilbert space, no\n  pure/mixed GNS tensor splitting, no spatial von Neumann tensor product, no\n  factor classification, and no manuscript state identity.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `lake build AristotleCandidates.VacuumRestrictedInfiniteTensorProduct` —\n  passed in the already-built live project (8026 jobs).\n- `lake build AristotleCandidates.VacuumRestrictedInfiniteTensorProduct Formalization`\n  — passed in the live project.\n- `lake build` — passed in the live project.\n- `#print axioms` for `exists_gram_coords`,\n  `exists_finiteMode_realization`, `sum_prod_inner_nonneg`,\n  `kern_eq_prod_of_subset`, `itpInner_nonneg`, `itpCore`,\n  `itpInner_vacuumVector`, and `exists_finiteMode_realization_kern` —\n  each reports only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound`.\n- A forbidden-declaration scan found no `sorry`, `admit`, user\n  `axiom`, `opaque`, `unsafe`, `implemented_by`, or\n  `native_decide`; the only textual matches are explanatory prose.\n- Direct-import audit: exactly `import Mathlib`; no external-project import.\n  `git diff --check` for the integrated module passed.\n\n## Adversarial review\n\nNo acceptance-blocking finding.  The review checked that the normalization\npremise is used to justify only padding outside the finite active set, that\npositivity reduces the complete finite Finsupp support to an actual finite\ncoordinate norm square, and that the `PreInnerProductSpace.Core` receives\nthe real-part nonnegativity required by its interface.  It also checked the\nempty-active-set vacuum calculation separately, so its norm-square-one\nstatement is not being promoted to a general product-padding theorem without\n`hΩ`.  Builds and axiom reports validate the formal declarations, but do\nnot create the intentionally omitted quotient, completion, GNS, or spatial\noperator-algebra results.\n\n## Cleanup and worktree state\n\nKept intentionally: the integrated P57A candidate, this closeout review, the\nexact raw archive, and its quarantine extraction.  The repository already had\nthe listed modified plan/task files and untracked earlier candidates/reviews;\nthey were not overwritten or otherwise changed.  No commit, push, archive\nbuild, dependency update, dependency fetch, or manuscript modification was\nperformed.\n# Manuscript source contract\n\n## Authoritative source\n\n- Manuscript: `sources/manuscript/main.tex`\n- Title in source: *Infinite Magic and the Continuum* (`main.tex:117`)\n- Formalization policy: `AGENTS.md`\n\nThis project does not treat its own Lean files, prior agent prompts, or the\nsurrounding repository as mathematical source authority.\n\n## Source clauses in scope\n\n| Source locator | What the manuscript says | Formalization status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `main.tex:1671-1679` | Defines short-range order through a local symplectic basis, with `W_Gamma = span{z_i : i in P}` and finite support. | Requires a precise Lean model of the infinite Pauli/symplectic data. |\n| `main.tex:1740-1752` | States the short-range regional-algebra theorem, with an asserted factorization `A_Gamma` into a pure-sector type-I factor and a core factor that is finite type I or hyperfinite `II_1`. | Target statement; not established by current Lean artifacts. |\n| `main.tex:1915-1920` | Proposes an adapted symplectic basis and a star-isomorphism from an infinite Pauli algebra to `A_Gamma`. | External assumption/gap until the source construction is formalized. |\n| `main.tex:1927-1931` | Writes a pure/mixed product density state. | Finite density-matrix fragments are formalized; the infinite model is not. |\n| `main.tex:1945-1967` | Proposes the GNS/tensor-factor and type conclusions. | Conditional GNS transport is formalized; analytic product and type conclusions are not. |\n| `main.tex:1970-2004` | Proposes quasi-equivalence and normal-folium transport. | Not formalized. |\n\nThe wording above is a locator-oriented paraphrase, not a replacement for the\nmanuscript.\n\n## Permitted auxiliary assumptions\n\nAny manuscript-facing Lean theorem that needs one of the following must expose\nit in the theorem statement or task packet:\n\n1. a completed C-star or von Neumann algebra model for the infinite Pauli\n   system and its regional algebra;\n2. the asserted star-isomorphism from that model to the regional algebra;\n3. the product-state formula and hypotheses making the GNS representation a\n   spatial tensor product;\n4. the von Neumann closure, normality, and quasi-equivalence assumptions needed\n   to transport the claimed factor type; and\n5. any hyperfiniteness or trace theorem used for the core factor.\n\nNone of these assumptions is supplied merely by the phrase “short-range\norder” in the current manuscript source.\n\n## Trace correction\n\nThe manuscript-facing target must distinguish two cases:\n\n- if the pure-sector Hilbert space is finite-dimensional, a normalized tracial\n  state can be obtained from the finite matrix trace together with the core\n  trace;\n- if the pure-sector Hilbert space is infinite-dimensional, the appropriate\n  conclusion is a faithful normal semifinite trace/weight on the `II_infinity`\n  target, not a tracial state.\n\nThis is a correction to the intended theorem contract, not a claim that the\ncurrent manuscript already states or proves the corrected dichotomy.\n\n## Legacy firewall\n\nEarlier gLU/refinement/layer-coherence material is listed in\n`CONTEXT-LEDGER.md`. It is retained, but it is neither a source clause nor an\nimplicit assumption of this formalization.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
